package webui

// The maintenance page: aggregate state only. Nothing here identifies a
// book, a file or an account — the question it answers is "is anything
// stuck?", and a count answers it (ADR-0013).

templ adminMaintenanceBody(prefix string, v maintenanceView, flash Flash) {
	<h2>Maintenance</h2>
	<p class="muted">
		What this server is reflecting, as the database sees it. Nothing
		here is a job to start or a queue to drain: a folder is read when
		it changes and again on a slow timer. If a count looks wrong,
		<a href={ settingsAdminHref(prefix, "folders") }>Folders</a> has a Scan now for
		each one — which is what a network share needs, because the kernel
		does not report changes on those.
	</p>
	@adminFlash(flash)
	<section class="card">
		<h2>Folders</h2>
		<dl class="facts">
			<dt>Watched</dt>
			<dd>{ i2s(v.Counts.Folders) }</dd>
			for _, row := range v.Kinds {
				<dt>{ row.Label }</dt>
				<dd>{ i2s(row.Count) }</dd>
			}
		</dl>
		<p class="muted">
			A folder's kind is read off the disk rather than chosen: a tree
			holding a Calibre database is a Calibre folder, and everything
			else is a plain one. They are listed, with their roots, on
			<a href={ settingsAdminHref(prefix, "folders") }>Folders</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="card">
		<h2>Books</h2>
		<dl class="facts">
			for _, row := range v.Books {
				<dt>{ row.Label }</dt>
				<dd>{ i2s(row.Count) }</dd>
			}
		</dl>
		<p class="muted">
			A missing book is one the last completed pass could not find on
			disk. Its row stays, with everybody's reading positions against
			it, and it comes back on its own if the file does — so a few are
			ordinary, and a great many at once usually mean a folder's disk
			is not mounted where it was.
		</p>
	</section>
}
